# SYNOPSIS

```usage
flock [-nosux] file cmd [arg ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`flock` opens <file>, creating it, mode 0644 less the umask, if it does not exist, takes a lock on it with the C library's `flock`, and runs <cmd> with the <arg>s, found in the directories of `PATH`, as a child.
When <cmd> exits `flock` closes <file>, which lets the lock go, and exits as <cmd> did.
By default it takes an exclusive lock, and waits until it can.

: `-x`
  Take an exclusive lock, the default.
: `-s`
  Take a shared lock.
: `-u`
  Remove a lock instead of taking one.
: `-n`
  Do not wait: if the lock is held elsewhere, exit 1 at once without running <cmd>.
: `-o`
  Close <file> in the child before running <cmd>, so the lock is not held by <cmd> nor by anything it leaves running.

`flock` is sbase's, and found only in the POSIX namespace: `/boot/bin/posix/flock` is a hard link to `sbase-box`, which runs the command its name names, and `/lib/ns/posix` (a manifest's `ns=posix`, see namespace(6)) binds `/boot/bin/posix` before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program that runs `flock` finds it there.
There is no native `flock`.

# SEE ALSO

lock(5); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/flock.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

Messages go to standard error, prefixed `flock: `, with the system's reason, as `flock: flock: No locks available`.
`flock` exits 1 when it cannot open <file>, lock it, start <cmd> or wait for it, and 1 at once with no message when `-n` finds the lock held.
A <cmd> that is not found makes the child exit 127, and one that cannot be run 126.
A <cmd> killed by a signal makes `flock` exit 128 and the signal's number; otherwise it exits as <cmd> did.
A bad flag, or fewer than two arguments, prints the usage message and exits 1.

# BUGS

The C library's `flock` is a lock on the whole file owned by the open file description, carried by 9Px's Tlock (lock(5)), on a server with the `posix` extension only (tmpfs, fsd(4), dosfs(4)); elsewhere it fails with `No locks available`.
A lock taken before a `fork` is the parent's alone; flock's and fcntl's locks on one file are kept in one table, so each is in the other's way, as on the BSDs, not Linux; and the server lets go of all a process's locks on a file when any of its opens of it is closed (lock(5)).

`-n` exits 1 when the lock is held, the same status as any error, so a script cannot tell the two apart.
